      "h1": "Rhode Island Squirrel Removal",
      "heroSub": "Our crew handles squirrel problems across Rhode Island — humane removal, proper sealing, and cleanup — Nuisance Wildlife Control Specialist (NWCS)-permitted, insured, with same-day service and 24/7 availability.",
      "leadParagraph": "<p>Squirrel problems in Rhode Island follow a northern calendar, and where you live in the state changes the job. Gray squirrels run the Providence metro's street-tree canopy from roof to roof, and the city's triple-deckers, along with older homes in Pawtucket, Woonsocket, and Cranston, give them soffit and dormer entries at every floor. Rhode Island squirrels raise litters in late winter and summer, and the state's sharpest attic-entry wave is the one its own season note predicts — fall, as animals prepare for winter and look for a warm void. Older housing helps them — soft fascia, uncapped chimneys, stone foundations, and loosened vents give squirrels openings newer construction does not. Call us and our crew will inspect first, explain what we found plainly, and handle removal and sealing as one job.</p>",
      "extendedBody": "<h2>Where Squirrels Are a Problem in Rhode Island</h2>\n<p>Gray squirrels run the Providence metro's street-tree canopy from roof to roof, and the city's triple-deckers, along with older homes in Pawtucket, Woonsocket, and Cranston, give them soffit and dormer entries at every floor. Warwick and Barrington's manicured suburbs add heavy roofline pressure, Kent and Washington counties add red and flying squirrels in the wooded interior of Coventry, West Greenwich, and Hopkinton, and Newport's historic homes offer old, soft trim that a gray can open in an afternoon.</p>\n<p>Rhode Island's coastal New England winters drive animals into older, tightly packed housing, while humid summers around Narragansett Bay fuel fast breeding in suburbs, mill cities, and shore communities alike. An opening, den, or tunnel system active this month is usually busier next month, so early inspection is the cheapest version of this job, from the Providence metro, Warwick and Kent County, South County's coast, and the islands of Newport County.</p>\n<h2>Squirrel Behavior, Damage, and Health Risks in Rhode Island</h2>\n<p>Rhode Island squirrels raise litters in late winter and summer, and the state's sharpest attic-entry wave is the one its own season note predicts — fall, as animals prepare for winter and look for a warm void.</p>\n<p>Eastern gray squirrels are the usual attic animal, with red squirrels in conifer country and southern flying squirrels — small, nocturnal, and often mistaken for rats — turning up in older houses. Grays breed twice in a northern climate, with litters in late winter and again in summer, so timing matters: sealing while a female is nursing hidden young walls them inside. Daytime running and rolling sounds overhead usually mean grays; scratching only after dark points to flying squirrels, and the exclusion plan is different for each.</p>\n<p>Squirrels gnaw constantly to wear down ever-growing teeth, and in an attic that means wood, stored items, and electrical wiring — a genuine fire concern in older housing. They shred insulation for nests, stain ceilings with urine, and widen a finger-width gap at a soffit or gable vent into a doorway in a few days. Because a squirrel needs little more than an inch of opening, a useful inspection checks the whole roofline, dormers, and roof intersections, not just the hole you can see from the ground.</p>\n<h2>Rhode Island Rules for Squirrel Work</h2>\n<p>Gray, red, and flying squirrels are all NWCS-authorized species in Rhode Island, so squirrel work runs under our NWCS permit. Rhode Island bars moving a live-caught mammal off the property, which suits squirrels perfectly: the standard job here has always been one-way exclusion, where the animal leaves on its own, is released in effect on its own home ground, and is sealed out with steel.</p>\n<p>We hold a Rhode Island Nuisance Wildlife Control Specialist (NWCS) permit from DEM and follow DEM rules on every job. We are insured, we offer same-day service when a call cannot wait, and we are available 24/7. If a job falls outside what Rhode Island allows, we will tell you on the first call and point you to RI DEM.</p>\n<h2>What a Rhode Island Squirrel Removal Job Includes</h2>\n<ul class=\"wildlife-list\">\n  <li><strong>Inspection and species ID</strong> — confirming gray, red, or flying squirrels, mapping every roofline entry, and checking for a nursing litter.</li>\n  <li><strong>One-way exclusion</strong> — fitting one-way doors at the main entries so squirrels leave to feed and cannot get back in.</li>\n  <li><strong>Steel sealing</strong> — closing soffits, gable and ridge vents, and construction gaps with galvanized steel and chew-proof screening.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cleanup</strong> — removing nest material and fouled insulation and sanitizing the affected attic areas.</li>\n  <li><strong>Prevention</strong> — trimming back limbs that bridge onto the roof and flagging chewed wiring for a licensed electrician to check.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every job ends the same way: the animals are out, the openings or burrows are closed, the mess they left is cleaned up, and you know exactly what we found and what we did. That finish — not a trap checked once and a hole left open — is what keeps squirrels from becoming a twice-a-year expense in Rhode Island.</p>",
