    "dead-animal-removal": {
      "index": true,
      "metaTitle": "Rhode Island Dead Animal Removal | NWCS-Permitted Local Crew Statewide",
      "metaDescription": "Dead Animal removal in Rhode Island — our crew removes dead animals, seals entries, and cleans up the mess they leave. NWCS-Permitted, Insured, same-day service available.",
      "h1": "Rhode Island Dead Animal Removal",
      "heroSub": "Our crew handles dead animal problems across Rhode Island — humane removal, proper sealing, and cleanup — Nuisance Wildlife Control Specialist (NWCS)-permitted, insured, with same-day service and 24/7 availability.",
      "leadParagraph": "<p>Dead Animal problems in Rhode Island follow a northern calendar, and where you live in the state changes the job. Rhode Island's calls cluster in its oldest housing — triple-deckers in Providence, Pawtucket, and Woonsocket, and shore homes opened after winter. Fall and winter deaths in wall voids lead, matching Rhode Island's fall peak for animals moving inside, and spring openings of shore and island homes produce the second wave. Older housing helps them — soft fascia, uncapped chimneys, stone foundations, and loosened vents give dead animals openings newer construction does not. Call us and our crew will inspect first, explain what we found plainly, and handle removal and sealing as one job.</p>",
      "extendedBody": "<h2>Where Dead animals Are a Problem in Rhode Island</h2>\n<p>Rhode Island's dead-animal calls cluster where its housing is oldest and tightest: squirrels and raccoons in chimneys and walls in Providence, Pawtucket, and Woonsocket triple-deckers, rats in basements along the Blackstone and Providence waterfronts, animals under the low decks and in the crawlspaces of Warwick and South County shore homes, and — every spring on Aquidneck and in the salt-pond communities — a house opened after winter with a carcass that has been in a wall since fall.</p>\n<p>Rhode Island's coastal New England winters drive animals into older, tightly packed housing, while humid summers around Narragansett Bay fuel fast breeding in suburbs, mill cities, and shore communities alike. An opening, den, or tunnel system active this month is usually busier next month, so early inspection is the cheapest version of this job, from the Providence metro, Warwick and Kent County, South County's coast, and the islands of Newport County.</p>\n<h2>Dead Animal Behavior, Damage, and Health Risks in Rhode Island</h2>\n<p>Fall and winter deaths in wall voids lead, matching Rhode Island's fall peak for animals moving inside, and spring openings of shore and island homes produce the second wave.</p>\n<p>The usual call is an odor with no visible cause: something has died in a wall void, attic, crawlspace, chimney, or under a deck or tub, and the smell gets worse for days before it gets better. In these northern states, fall and winter are the peak — animals that moved inside for warmth die in hidden voids — but a raccoon in a chimney in spring or a squirrel in a wall in any season is the same job. Flies gathering at one window or one wall are often the best clue to where the carcass is.</p>\n<p>Finding the animal is most of the work. Our crew uses the odor pattern, fly activity, and the structure itself to narrow the location, and where a wall or ceiling has to be opened, we open as small an area as we can and tell you before we cut. We remove the carcass with protective equipment — carcasses of rabies-vector animals are handled with particular care and never bare-handed — then disinfect and deodorize the cavity, and replace fouled insulation where it has absorbed the odor. Just as important, we find how the animal got in, because a dead animal indoors almost always means a live-animal entry point that is still open.</p>\n<h2>Rhode Island Rules for Dead Animal Work</h2>\n<p>Carcass work sits inside Rhode Island's NWCS framework — moving a dead animal off a property for disposal is expressly contemplated by the rules, unlike moving a live one, which is barred. What governs our handling is the rabies list: raccoon, skunk, bat, woodchuck, and fox carcasses get protective equipment and no bare-hand contact, and any human contact with a potentially rabid animal is reported to the Department of Health right away.</p>\n<p>We hold a Rhode Island Nuisance Wildlife Control Specialist (NWCS) permit from DEM and follow DEM rules on every job. We are insured, we offer same-day service when a call cannot wait, and we are available 24/7. If a job falls outside what Rhode Island allows, we will tell you on the first call and point you to RI DEM.</p>\n<h2>What a Rhode Island Dead Animal Removal Job Includes</h2>\n<ul class=\"wildlife-list\">\n  <li><strong>Location work</strong> — narrowing the carcass location from odor, flies, and building layout before anything is opened.</li>\n  <li><strong>Removal</strong> — retrieving the carcass from attics, walls, crawlspaces, chimneys, and under structures, with protective equipment.</li>\n  <li><strong>Disinfection and deodorizing</strong> — treating the cavity or area and removing insulation that has absorbed the odor.</li>\n  <li><strong>Small, honest openings</strong> — cutting only what is needed to reach the animal, and telling you before we open a wall or ceiling.</li>\n  <li><strong>Entry-point check</strong> — finding and sealing how the animal got inside, so the next one does not die in the same place.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every job ends the same way: the animals are out, the openings or burrows are closed, the mess they left is cleaned up, and you know exactly what we found and what we did. That finish — not a trap checked once and a hole left open — is what keeps dead animals from becoming a twice-a-year expense in Rhode Island.</p>",
      "faqs": [
        {
          "q": "There is a dead-animal smell in our Rhode Island shore house after winter. Where do you start?",
          "a": "With the chimney, the wall voids over the warmest rooms, and any crawlspace the house sat over all winter — in a closed shore house, odor concentrates and travels, so we work from fly activity and the strongest point rather than opening walls at random. We also check how the animal got in before you close the house up again."
        },
        {
          "q": "What are the Rhode Island rules for dead animal removal?",
          "a": "Carcass work sits inside Rhode Island's NWCS framework — moving a dead animal off a property for disposal is expressly contemplated by the rules, unlike moving a live one, which is barred. What governs our handling is the rabies list: raccoon, skunk, bat, woodchuck, and fox carcasses get protective equipment and no bare-hand contact, and any human contact with a potentially rabid animal is reported to the Department of Health right away."
        },
        {
          "q": "How much does dead animal removal cost in Rhode Island?",
          "a": "It depends on the job. A straightforward dead animal job costs less than one that needs full exclusion, cleanup, decontamination, and repair, and typical ranges vary by how many animals are present, how many entry points we have to seal, and how much damage they have done. We quote after we inspect your home, so you see exactly what we found before you decide."
        }
      ]
    }
